@using Memoria.Web.Extensibility

@* The declared type of the instance a detail page is showing, over the page rather than instead of
   it: what a reader wants of the type while reading one of its rows is a glance at what the shape
   is meant to be, and leaving for the types page to get it means coming back to find the row
   again.

   Open because the address says so and closed by a link that takes that back, like every other view
   on these pages. No script and no <dialog>: these pages render statically, the framework's modal
   needs JavaScript to open at all, and a reader who followed a link here can leave the way they
   came — the close is a link, the backdrop behind it is the same link, and the browser's own back
   button undoes the whole thing. *@
<div class="overlay" id="@Anchor" tabindex="-1">
    @* The way out that a click anywhere outside is. Kept out of the reading order and out of the
       accessibility tree: it is the same address as the Close beside the title, and offered twice
       it would be read out twice. *@
    <a class="overlay-dismiss" href="@CloseLink" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"></a>

    @* Named by its heading rather than marked aria-modal: nothing here traps focus or hides the
       page behind it from a screen reader, and saying otherwise would promise a reader something
       that is not true. What it is instead is a region with a name, which is what it behaves as. *@
    <section class="dialog" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="@(Anchor)-title">
        <header class="dialog-header">
            @* The mark the types pages are entered by, in front of the name — the same mark the
               button that opened this carries, so what was clicked and what arrived look like the
               same thing. *@
            <h2 id="@(Anchor)-title">
                <SectionMark Kind="SectionKind.Types"/>
                @DomainTypeDescriber.LabelOf(Description.Type)
            </h2>

            <a class="dialog-close" href="@CloseLink" aria-label="Close" title="Close">&times;</a>
        </header>

        <div class="dialog-body">
            @* The same four views the types pages show, drawn by the same component: a reader
               reaching a type from a stored row and a reader reaching it from the list of types are
               reading the same thing, and there is one place to change it. The tabs carry their own
               ids, so they do not collide with the page's own row of them underneath. *@
            <TypeView Description="Description"
                      Kind="Kind"
                      Store="Store"
                      Tab="@Tab"
                      TabLink="TabLink"
                      DataLink="DataLink"
                      Prefix="@($"{Anchor}-tab")"/>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {

    /// <summary>The type being shown, described.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public DomainTypeDescription Description { get; set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>Which of the two models it is.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public ModelKind Kind { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which store folds it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public ModelStore Store { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which of the four views is open.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Tab { get; set; } = TypeView.InfoTab;

    /// <summary>Where each of them leads, which is this page with another view asked for.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public Func<string, string> TabLink { get; set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>This page with nothing asked for, which is what closes it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string CloseLink { get; set; } = null!;

    /// <summary>
    /// Through to what has been stored under one identifier, or null to leave the column out.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<Type, string>? DataLink { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What this is called in the address, so the link that opens it can land on it and the ids
    /// inside it have something of their own to be prefixed by.
    /// </summary>
    public const string Anchor = "type";

}
